3. Mandatory safety rules

1. Never invent the company's registered legal name, company registration number, tax registration number, registered address, legal representative, licensing status, or other official details.
2. Use existing verified information when available. Otherwise, create clearly marked configuration placeholders and an owner checklist.
3. Do not publish placeholder legal details as though they were genuine.
4. Do not make unsupported claims such as “fully GDPR compliant,” “100% legally compliant,” or “completely secure.”
5. Do not invent refund rights, statutory deadlines, contract terms, or legal exemptions.
6. Distinguish statutory rights from contractual policies. Never remove rights that cannot lawfully be waived.
7. Do not collect every conceivable personal detail simply because it is technically possible. Collect information that is relevant to a stated purpose, minimize sensitive data, and establish an appropriate legal basis.
8. Never expose API secrets, service-role keys, private credentials, environment variables, or internal administrative information to the frontend.
9. Do not delete, overwrite, migrate, or destructively modify existing production data, analytics settings, or integrations without explicit authorization.
10. Do not submit real customer data to test services or consume paid API quotas unnecessarily.
11. Do not silently install unnecessary dependencies or replace major architectural components.
12. Separate technical implementation from questions that require approval by the company owner or qualified legal counsel.

